WEBVTT

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:01.010
So let's say

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.110
as an example we sit down

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:07.159
and we're at our computer at home and we go to launch New York Times,

00:00:07.199 --> 00:00:08.729
HTTPS New York Times,

00:00:08.879 --> 00:00:10.270
NYTimes.com.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.080
What happens between you hitting enter.

00:00:13.629 --> 00:00:15.449
And that website appearing

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:16.489
on your,

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:17.909
on your browser of choice,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.139
right?

00:00:18.199 --> 00:00:18.620
How do we,

00:00:18.659 --> 00:00:19.540
how do we get there?

00:00:19.979 --> 00:00:22.729
The start of our journey is with a web browser.

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.699
So what is what is a web browser,

00:00:24.739 --> 00:00:25.209
right?

00:00:25.659 --> 00:00:26.409
It's really

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:27.939
a program like any other.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.770
You have hundreds of programs on your computer,

00:00:29.899 --> 00:00:31.689
a web browser is one of them.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.760
It doesn't necessarily

00:00:34.149 --> 00:00:35.370
have to be anything special.

00:00:35.459 --> 00:00:39.449
It is just a program that specifically speaks HTTP.

00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:45.310
It trades in writing HTTP queries to send out or request to send out.

00:00:45.439 --> 00:00:49.120
It receives HTTP responses and then renders those and displays

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.760
them on your computer so you can interact with them.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.159
So what does that browser do?

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:56.439
Beyond making that communication with those web servers

00:00:56.439 --> 00:00:57.830
that are out there on the internet,

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:58.159
uh,

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:00.870
it's going to translate a lot of that,

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:01.370
um,

00:01:01.479 --> 00:01:04.270
fairly ugly behind the scenes computer speak

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.310
into a beautiful functional website that you are used to interacting with.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.169
So in doing so,

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:16.840
the first thing we're gonna need to do is we're going to need to draft an HTTP request,

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.489
meaning we need to ask a question of a computer

00:01:19.489 --> 00:01:22.159
somewhere else in the world to say please send me

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.100
the homepage for NewYorkTimes.com.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:26.840
So we're gonna draft this request,

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:27.139
uh,

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.520
and there are a number of pieces of this

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:30.959
that we'll individually talk about.

00:01:31.169 --> 00:01:32.060
So first we have a method,

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:32.449
right?

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:33.729
So we have get,

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:34.810
we want to get

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:35.679
a file.

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:37.250
Sounds just like what it is.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.090
We're asking to receive something.

00:01:39.669 --> 00:01:39.870
Uh,

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:40.860
we have

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:41.550
a,

00:01:41.709 --> 00:01:41.879
uh,

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:43.540
we have a path that we're looking to receive.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.310
In this case we're using just Slash,

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:46.429
which is the home page.

00:01:46.469 --> 00:01:48.179
We'll get into a bit more of that a bit later.

00:01:48.629 --> 00:01:51.219
And then what protocol and version are we using?

00:01:51.429 --> 00:01:53.149
So specifically we're using HTTP.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.679
This reference is version 2.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:56.469
We will come back to that a little bit and talk about

00:01:56.469 --> 00:01:59.989
a few different versions of HTTP and why that might be important.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:01.189
We will get back to that in a second.

00:02:01.709 --> 00:02:01.949
Uh,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.909
and then also we have potentially a whole

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:07.110
number of headers separated with their values.

00:02:07.149 --> 00:02:11.870
Now in a real request there's this is gonna be a long off off the page several headers

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.590
and several different values for simplicity we're boiling it

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:16.469
down to really the host we're looking for.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:17.659
This is certainly required.

00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:21.229
We want New York Times.com or NYTimes.com.

00:02:21.429 --> 00:02:23.459
There may be several other header values

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:27.379
um that that are all gonna do different things and maybe specify a bit more

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:29.270
what we are requesting.

00:02:30.479 --> 00:02:32.389
So we're gonna send that request over the

00:02:32.389 --> 00:02:34.600
internet and just to reinforce this concept,

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.419
all those other protocols we talked about,

00:02:36.429 --> 00:02:37.899
those are still in play here,

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:38.229
right?

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:39.340
If we want to know

00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:42.540
how to send a message to New York Times or NYTimes.com,

00:02:42.949 --> 00:02:46.050
we need to use DNS to figure out what address we're sending that to.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.669
We need to look them up in the phone book,

00:02:47.949 --> 00:02:48.080
right?

00:02:48.189 --> 00:02:49.620
Then we need to break that down

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:53.110
into into packets and send it out with TCPIP and make

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:55.699
sure that it's been acknowledged and they've received our request.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:56.229
We need to.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.270
Use routing tools because who knows where in the

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.300
world and why time might actually be hosted,

00:03:00.469 --> 00:03:00.590
right?

00:03:00.669 --> 00:03:02.060
We have to send that data

00:03:02.419 --> 00:03:04.739
around the world and and to all these different computers

00:03:04.989 --> 00:03:06.949
to make sure that it receives at its destination.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:07.500
So just

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.570
remember that all these things that we've talked about,

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:11.629
they're still happening behind the scenes they're

00:03:11.629 --> 00:03:13.669
just slightly lower level those are building blocks

00:03:13.669 --> 00:03:15.870
that we're we're abstracting away from and we're

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.820
doing bigger and and more powerful things,

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:21.989
but we're still using those core building blocks uh to get through our journey here.

00:03:22.899 --> 00:03:24.699
So the server is gonna receive our message

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:27.590
and it's going to need to do something with it.

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:29.870
So the first thing it's gonna do is say,

00:03:29.929 --> 00:03:30.290
well,

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.429
what software is this message

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:33.669
intended for?

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.760
Earlier we talked about TCP port numbers,

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:35.959
right?

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:36.050
And,

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.080
and how we can have different conversations on the phone

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.469
so we're not hearing other people on the same line.

00:03:40.719 --> 00:03:41.669
This is more of the same

00:03:41.919 --> 00:03:44.399
because we requested an HTTPS website,

00:03:44.409 --> 00:03:47.510
which is which is an encrypted or secure HTTP website.

00:03:47.719 --> 00:03:49.760
This likely came over on port 443.

00:03:50.219 --> 00:03:51.409
So the computer's going to say,

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:51.600
Well,

00:03:51.729 --> 00:03:53.120
I have a request in 443.

00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:57.280
I'm supposed to forward this to a piece of software called a web server.

00:03:57.409 --> 00:03:59.949
So it forwards that request along and the web server

00:03:59.949 --> 00:04:02.760
in turn now receives this entire message and says,

00:04:02.889 --> 00:04:03.080
Ah,

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:04.300
I I have a new message.

00:04:04.449 --> 00:04:06.469
What am I doing with this data?

00:04:06.729 --> 00:04:09.239
I see that someone would like to get a specific file,

00:04:09.409 --> 00:04:10.649
particularly our homepage.

00:04:11.169 --> 00:04:11.290
Uh,

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:14.639
it's important to note there are other request types that we could make.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:17.679
The most common we will see is we're asking to download,

00:04:17.929 --> 00:04:18.050
right?

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:19.500
We just give me your home page,

00:04:19.689 --> 00:04:21.229
show me the data that you have,

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:21.610
uh,

00:04:21.699 --> 00:04:23.729
but perhaps we want to send information.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.810
Say for example you're making an order on some

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.040
online shopping website and it's time to check out.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:30.520
You'll likely put your address,

00:04:30.649 --> 00:04:30.989
your name,

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:31.409
your address,

00:04:31.489 --> 00:04:32.489
where you're shipping it to,

00:04:32.649 --> 00:04:34.679
perhaps payment information like a credit card,

00:04:34.909 --> 00:04:37.889
and you'll submit that and we're sending that information.

00:04:38.199 --> 00:04:42.120
To this server ultimately to do something we wish it to do like

00:04:42.399 --> 00:04:45.459
charge our credit card purchase the the object we're trying to buy,

00:04:45.489 --> 00:04:50.129
and then put it in the uh in the mail shipping it to us so we can both receive and send and

00:04:50.129 --> 00:04:53.620
indeed do many other things in HTTP that it would support

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.770
primarily get is the easiest to talk about because it's simple,

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:57.010
right?

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:58.360
We're just downloading a file,

00:04:58.530 --> 00:04:59.889
give me this data.

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:01.250
Uh,

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:02.350
and then finally,

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:08.090
We are going to draft the HTTP response and send that back.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.850
So much like the browser needed to put together this text

00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:14.770
to send off to the web server to interpret,

00:05:15.179 --> 00:05:16.409
we may have the file,

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:18.860
but that file needs to be wrapped in some sort of

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.969
protocol so the browser knows what to do with it.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:24.459
So it's going to write an HTTP response that might look something like this.

00:05:25.409 --> 00:05:28.439
We'll still have our HTTP 2 which gives us the version.

00:05:28.729 --> 00:05:28.850
Uh,

00:05:28.889 --> 00:05:30.179
we have 200,

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:31.810
kind of a strange number to have there,

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:32.239
and we'll say,

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:32.409
OK,

00:05:32.489 --> 00:05:33.409
we'll get back to that in a minute.

00:05:34.389 --> 00:05:35.030
Something like the date,

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:38.139
these are other headers and values we have a date like the current date and time

00:05:38.469 --> 00:05:39.550
and then content type.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.320
So here we might have text slash HTML and that what that's

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.639
doing is it's giving our browser a bit of a hint.

00:05:46.739 --> 00:05:49.090
Because there's a whole bunch of different files we could request.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.169
Think about the type of content that you look at

00:05:51.379 --> 00:05:53.219
when you're on your web browser.

00:05:53.239 --> 00:05:54.139
You might watch videos,

00:05:54.219 --> 00:05:55.449
you might listen to audio,

00:05:55.659 --> 00:05:57.139
you might be images that you're loading,

00:05:57.149 --> 00:05:57.350
right?

00:05:57.379 --> 00:06:01.689
In this case we have an HTML site or a basically a home page that's gonna be rendered.

00:06:01.899 --> 00:06:04.899
It's possible you could be downloading a PDF or an Excel file or who knows,

00:06:04.979 --> 00:06:06.709
however many other types of files.

00:06:06.939 --> 00:06:08.379
So we're going to give the browser a bit of a.

00:06:08.459 --> 00:06:08.899
Hint

00:06:09.129 --> 00:06:12.489
saying this is the type of data I'm gonna be sending you

00:06:12.489 --> 00:06:15.489
so when the browser receives this response it has an idea of

00:06:15.489 --> 00:06:18.199
what to do with it because the browsers have gotten pretty powerful

00:06:18.199 --> 00:06:22.000
and they actually know how to delegate what they do based on

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:23.280
the type that they're receiving,

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:23.449
right?

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:26.969
They might try to open that file in Excel for you or maybe render that PDF

00:06:26.969 --> 00:06:30.330
in a different way or open up uh some other piece of software to render that.

00:06:30.449 --> 00:06:33.479
PDF for you so we give the browser some hints

00:06:33.479 --> 00:06:36.040
and just like in the request there are far other

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:39.639
headers that aren't mentioned here that give those browser additional

00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:42.750
hints to give them clues on how they should behave

00:06:42.959 --> 00:06:44.830
in this continued back and forth

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:46.030
uh conversation

00:06:46.199 --> 00:06:49.840
where machines are speaking to other machines and then finally of course

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.320
we will send the file itself we requested the home page.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:55.239
Here is the the actual content of the home page.
